  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The Athletics trailed early but turned it with two runs in the fifth, then hung on through nine innings to extend the streak to two straight wins.",
    "what_this_means": "Jack Perkins lasted only three innings but the bullpen held up after that. Blewett threw two clean innings, and the back end shut the door with Medina and Harris combining for two scoreless frames in high leverage. The offense got 14 hits and converted four runners-in-scoring-position situations into runs, which is enough to matter against a team that had beaten them already. Zack Gelof's homer in the fifth was the swing that changed the game from chase to control. They led from inning five onward. Seattle never caught up. This is a team that doesn't need early rhythm to finish a game. They will find the moment if the bullpen gives them one. Two straight wins built on scratchy lineups and late execution. That's not sustainable, but it's not nothing either.",
    "what_to_watch": "Logan Gilbert comes in tonight at 3.42 ERA, which is clean, but Seattle's lineup is batting .229 as a team. Among the worst in baseball. Morris gets a favorable matchup if the A's can avoid early trouble.",
